  "id": "DrugBank_DB00338 - MONDO_2177",
  "verbalization": "Omeprazole, Roxatidine Acetate, Pantoprazole, and Dexrabeprazole are all proton pump inhibitors that inhibit gastric acid secretion, and they share a common mechanism of action involving the inhibition of the H+/K+ ATPase enzyme in gastric parietal cells. This mechanism is linked to the induction of gastrin secretion abnormalities, which in turn may contribute to the development of hyperinsulinism. The evidence suggests that Omeprazole, through its ability to reduce gastric acid secretion, may influence gastrin levels, potentially disrupting the normal regulation of insulin secretion. This disruption could lead to an overproduction of insulin, resulting in hyperinsulinism. The presence of multiple pathways connecting Omeprazole to gastrin secretion abnormalities and ultimately to hyperinsulinism highlights the potential for Omeprazole to be repurposed for the treatment of this condition. The integration of these pathways underscores the importance of targeting the gastrin-secretion axis in the management of hyperinsulinism.",
